clueweb09-en0005-00-32635.0  ÿ Health Department:Services and Programs:Health Initiatives:HEART Safe Community Program:Making Communities in Dutchess County HEART Safe Select a Department Aging Airport Board of Elections Budget Office Central Services (Purchasing) Comptroller Computer Information Systems, Office of Consumer Affairs County Attorney County Clerk County Executive County Legislature Criminal Justice Council District Attorney Economic Development Corporation Emergency Response Finance Health, Department of History Department Human Rights Commission Jail Long Term Care-DUTCHESS NY Connects Mass Transit (LOOP Bus System) Mental Hygiene Motor Vehicle Bureau Personnel Planning and Development Probation Public Defender Public Works Public Works - Parks Real Property Tax Resource Recovery Agency Risk Management Sheriff's Office Social Services Soil and Water Conservation Solid Waste Management STOP-DWI Traffic Safety Board Veterans Affairs Water and Wastewater Authority Youth Bureau [County Executive] Home . Search . County Government . Municipalities . Business . Schools . Tourism . Community . Elected Officials Text Size: [Normal Text Size] [Large
clueweb09-en0005-00-32635.135  . Schools . Tourism . Community . Elected Officials Text Size: [Normal Text Size] [Large Text Size] County Government >> Departments >> Department of Health >> Making Communities in Dutchess County HEART Safe Making Communities in Dutchess County HEART Safe [Department Head photo] Department of Health Michael C. Caldwell, MD, MPH, Commissioner [Dutchess County Government Cares] Dutchess County Government Cares (.pdf version) Department of Health Application Packet (.pdf) Application Process Dutchess County HEART Safe Community Application (.pdf) Additional Documents and Resources Approximately 140-190 residents in Dutchess County die each year due to sudden cardiac arrest. Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death in Dutchess County and in the nation. According to the American Heart Association less than five percent of sudden cardiac arrest victims survive because a vast majority witnessing the arrest do not know CPR or the chain of survival consisting of a series of four steps: Early
clueweb09-en0005-00-32635.270  know CPR or the chain of survival consisting of a series of four steps: Early access to care (dial 911 immediately), Early CPR to maintain blood flow to the brain until the next step, Early defibrillation to deliver a shock of electricity to restart the heart with the use of an AED, and Early advanced care to be administered by a pre-hospital emergency team at the scene and transport to the hospital. To help increase the survival of those who experience a heart attack in Dutchess County, County Executive William R. Steinhaus charged the Dutchess County Department of Health with the implementation of an American Heart Association program called HEART Safe Communities. The HEART Safe program is based on incentives and recognition to all those who meet specific criteria that have the potential for saving the lives of sudden cardiac arrest victims through the use of cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) and
clueweb09-en0005-00-32635.405  the lives of sudden cardiac arrest victims through the use of cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) and increased public access to defibrillation. A HEART Safe entity or community promotes and supports: CPR training in the community, Public access to defibrillation through strategic placement of automated external defibrillators (AEDs) for use by public safety professionals and other trained community members, Early advanced care, and Increase of out of hospital survival rate. Benefits of being a HEART Safe Community: There are various benefits to having a HEART Safe organizations or entity. First, it increases the chances of survival of those who might have a sudden cardiac arrest at a HEART Safe location. Second, having a compliant program including emergency plan, drills and training, will minimize the frivolous lawsuits and offer legal protection. And finally, worksites entities might have a reduction in their workers compensation insurance premium. Do I live in a HEART Safe Community?
clueweb09-en0007-85-36500.0 Dutchess County Tourism - Colleges & Cultural Centers W Search Events Calendar: Search Events by Location: All Amenia Annandale-on-Hudson Beacon Beekman Clinton Corners Dover Plains Fishkill Germantown Hopewell Junction Hyde Park LaGrange Lagrangeville Millbrook Millerton Pawling Pine Plains Pleasant Valley Poughkeepsie Poughquag Red Hook Rhinebeck Staatsburg Stanford Stormville Tivoli Wappingers Falls Wassaic Wingdale [Colleges & Cultural Centers] Colleges Group Tours Press Room Advertise With Us Contact DCT About DCT Home [College campus]Five institutions of higher learning call Dutchess home. Take advantage of theater & music offerings, art exhibits, sporting & special events. Each college is situated on a scenic campus. Also be sure to read more about the fine arts and cultural centers located in Dutchess County. Colleges/Education Bard College Description: A liberal arts college founded in 1860, 600-acre campus overlooking the Hudson River. Richard B. Fisher Center for the Performing Arts, designed by Frank Gehry, presents a variety of opera, music, drama, and dance performances. Special events
clueweb09-en0007-85-36500.135 Dutchess County Tourism - Colleges & Cultural Centers by Frank Gehry, presents a variety of opera, music, drama, and dance performances. Special events include the annual Bard Music Festival, conferences, lectures, and other public presentations. Center for Curatorial Studies presents exhibitions of contemporary art. Levy Econonmics Institute hosts conference and lectures on economic and policy issues. Location: Rt. 9G, Annandale-on-Hudson. Contact: 845/758-6822 URL: Bard.edu Codes: G, T, H The Culinary Institute of America Description: Bachelor's and associate degree programs for careers in the culinary arts, baking and pastry arts. 2,300 students. 800/285-4627. Continuing education programs for foodservice professionals, all year. Adult education courses for food enthusiasts, spring and fall. 800/888-7850. Five award-winning, student-staffed restaurants. Location: Rt. 9, Hyde Park. Contact: 845/452-9600, 845/471-6608 for reservations. See restaurant section. URL: CIAChef.edu Codes: G, T, H Dutchess Community College Description: Serves over 25,000 local residents each year through credit and credit-free classes and training for business and industry. Features 65 academic
clueweb09-en0007-85-36500.270 Dutchess County Tourism - Colleges & Cultural Centers year through credit and credit-free classes and training for business and industry. Features 65 academic programs, an art gallery, a theatre, and numerous cultural activities open to the public presented on campus throughout the year. Main campus on 130 acres in the Town of Poughkeepsie with an extension site on Myers Corners Road. Location: 53 Pendell Rd., Poughkeepsie. Contact: 845/431-8000 URL: SUNYDutchess.edu Codes: G, T, H Marist College Description: Liberal arts college on the Hudson River. 4000 undergraduate and 1000 graduate students; offers professional training to business, government and nonprofit organizations. Marist Art Gallery showcases Hudson Valley artists. Musicals, plays and lectures in the Nelly Goletti Theatre. Lowell Thomas Communications Center exhibits personal effects of the late broadcaster. Men's and women's NCAA Division I athletic contests on site as well as crew races on the Hudson River. Location: Rt. 9, Poughkeepsie. Contact: 845/575-3174 URL: Marist.edu Codes: G, T, H Vassar
clueweb09-en0007-85-36500.405 Dutchess County Tourism - Colleges & Cultural Centers Hudson River. Location: Rt. 9, Poughkeepsie. Contact: 845/575-3174 URL: Marist.edu Codes: G, T, H Vassar College Description: Liberal arts, co-ed college founded by brewer/philanthropist Matthew Vassar in 1861. Beautiful 1000-acre campus with 200 species of trees, two lakes, outdoor amphitheater, ecological preserve, Frances Lehman Loeb Art Center, Powerhouse Summer Theater, James W. Palmer Gallery, Skinner Hall of Music, and Class of 1951 Observatory. Meeting facilities available at campus Alumnae House inn/restaurant. Location: 124 Raymond Ave., Poughkeepsie. Contact: 845/437-7404 URL: Vassar.edu Codes: T, H, G, F About DCT | Advertise with Us | Contact Us | Privacy Policy | Terms of Use | Home (C) 2006 Dutchess County Tourism. All rights reserved. Dutchess County Tourism, 3 Neptune Rd. Suite Q-17, Poughkeepsie NY 12601 Tel: 845/463-4000 or 800/445-3131 The programs provided by this agency are partially funded by monies received from the County of Dutchess.
clueweb09-en0007-85-36517.0 Dutchess County Tourism >>Meeting & Event Spaces Back to Meetings Home Beekman Arms Delamater Inn & Conference Center The Beekman Arms, America’s oldest continuously operated hotel, is best suited for smaller group functions. Together with the Delamater Inn, it offers 73 rooms and a 40-person conference center, complete with ﬁreplace and French doors. Rhinebeck, NY 845/876-7077 [line] Best Western Inn & Conference Center The Best Western Inn & Conference Center is a full-service, 153-room hotel with restaurant, lounge and more than 11,000 square feet of function space. All rooms have high-speed Internet, and meeting and event planner support is available. Poughkeepsie, NY 845/462-4600 [line] Bright Horizons at Casperkill Indoor and outdoor recreational and conference facilities can available for small and large group outings. Rental space includes: Gymnasiums for youth or adult sports tournaments, clinics and special events; softball fields for tournaments and leagues; and versatile private rooms for diverse small and large group
clueweb09-en0007-85-36517.135 Dutchess County Tourism fields for tournaments and leagues; and versatile private rooms for diverse small and large group functions. [horizonsconfroom] Poughkeepsie 845/463-6437 [line] Mid-Hudson Civic Center Composed of McCann Ice Arena and Mair Hall, a 3,000 capacity venue for concerts, trade shows and banquets. Both are available to promoters, organizations and businesses. Event Space: 22,000+ square feet. Exhibit Space: 19,000 + square feet (hall and lobby). Breakout rooms: 8. [midhudsonciviccenter] Poughkeepsie 845/454-5800 [line] Poughkeepsie Grand Hotel The Poughkeepsie Grand, a full-service hotel and conference center with 200 guest rooms and more than 15,000 feet of ﬂexible space, offers wireless high-speed Internet, business center, complimentary All-American breakfast, indoor parking and local shuttle service. Poughkeepsie, NY 845/485-5300 [line] View Additional Spaces About DCT | Advertise with Us | Contact Us | Privacy Policy | Terms of Use | Home (C) 2006 Dutchess County Tourism. All rights reserved. Dutchess County Tourism, 3 Neptune Rd. Suite Q-17,
