clueweb09-en0005-00-32638.135  [Normal Text Size] [Large Text Size] County Government >> Departments >> Human Rights [Department Head Photo] Marilynn A. Vetrano, Executive Director Human Rights Commission 22 Market Street Poughkeepsie, NY 12601 Voice (845) 486-2445 Fax (845) 486-2468 e-Mail [GeoAccess Map Link Icon] Locate Us! Mission Statement The Dutchess County Human Rights Commission was established in 1984 to serve the residents of Dutchess County. Its primary objectives are: To foster a community climate in which better understanding and relationships among various racial, religious and ethnic groups can easily and naturally take place. To recommend and conduct educational programs and training to foster mutual respect and increase goodwill and understanding among residents of Dutchess County. To inquire into incidents of tension and conflicts among or between various racial, religious and nationality groups, and act to relieve this tension. To receive complaints of alleged discrimination, attempt a just solution and to seek assistance of
clueweb09-en0005-00-32638.270  To receive complaints of alleged discrimination, attempt a just solution and to seek assistance of the New York State Division of Human Rights in the solution of complaints that fall within its jurisdiction. Business Hours: 9:00 am - 5:00 pm Monday - Friday Phone: (845) 486-2445, Bilingual Staff (Spanish) COMMISSIONERS Dazell Green, Chairperson William V. Beehler Lawrence Fauntleroy Sharon Wellington-deAnda William Quinn Commission Meetings Monthly Commission meetings are held the 3rd Monday of the month, at 6:00 p.m. at the Commission Office, 22 Market Street, Basement, Poughkeepsie, New York. Meetings are open to the public. Any individual or community organization interested in an opportunity to discuss community concerns, should contact the Commission Office, 845-486-2445 and advise they will be attending. Go to top of page - Home - | - Legal Statements - | - How to Use this Site - | - Contact Us - Last Updated: 2/13/2008
clueweb09-en0005-00-32631.0  ÿ County Executive:County Executive's Biography Select a Department Aging Airport Board of Elections Budget Office Central Services (Purchasing) Comptroller Computer Information Systems, Office of Consumer Affairs County Attorney County Clerk County Executive County Legislature Criminal Justice Council District Attorney Economic Development Corporation Emergency Response Finance Health, Department of History Department Human Rights Commission Jail Long Term Care-DUTCHESS NY Connects Mass Transit (LOOP Bus System) Mental Hygiene Motor Vehicle Bureau Personnel Planning and Development Probation Public Defender Public Works Public Works - Parks Real Property Tax Resource Recovery Agency Risk Management Sheriff's Office Social Services Soil and Water Conservation Solid Waste Management STOP-DWI Traffic Safety Board Veterans Affairs Water and Wastewater Authority Youth Bureau Home . Search . County Government . Municipalities . Business . Schools . Tourism . Community . Elected Officials Text Size: [Normal Text Size] [Large Text Size] County Government >> Departments >> County Executive [County Executive] County
clueweb09-en0005-00-32631.135  Text Size] [Large Text Size] County Government >> Departments >> County Executive [County Executive] County Executive 22 Market. St., Poughkeepsie, NY 12601 Voice (845) 486-2000 Fax (845) 486-2021 e-Mail William R. Steinhaus, County Executive County Executive's Biography William R. Steinhaus was born on November 8, 1949. He is a graduate of Roy C. Ketcham High School in Wappingers Falls, New York, Dutchess Community College and SUNY Brockport, where he received a Bachelor of Science Degree in Political Science in 1972. Mr. Steinhaus continued his education through graduate studies at Bridgeport University and SUNY New Paltz. Mr. Steinhaus began his elected public service career in 1979 after his election at the age of 28 as Dutchess County Clerk and Commissioner of Motor Vehicles. He was re-elected in 1981, 1984, and 1987 and served in that position through 1991. In 1986, he was honored by the New York State Association of County
clueweb09-en0005-00-32631.270  through 1991. In 1986, he was honored by the New York State Association of County Clerks as "County Clerk of the Year." Elected County Executive in November 1991, he took office as the youngest County Executive in Dutchess County's history. Mr. Steinhaus captured nearly 70% of the vote. He was re-elected in 1995, in 1999, in 2003, and again in 2007, to an unprecedented fifth, four year term. He is now serving in his seventeenth year and is the longest serving County Executive in county history. Mr. Steinhaus has spearheaded reform in the Countys government including major department and service area consolidations. His wide-ranging cost containment initiatives have proven successful in the areas of criminal justice, health and human services, and administrative realignments. The Countys expanded service mission is being fulfilled with fewer county employees than in 1992 when he first became County Executive. Primary emphasis has been placed on
clueweb09-en0005-00-32631.405  than in 1992 when he first became County Executive. Primary emphasis has been placed on strong management and executive office leadership and on the promotion of fundamental change in the way the county conducts business. The first executive budget developed by Mr. Steinhaus produced a cut in real property taxes; the beginning of a record that achieved a county tax rate per $1,000 that is 73% lower in 2008 than it was in 1989. The County Executive has championed the communitys job creation efforts with a comprehensive series of community and economic development policy initiatives and strategies. The centerpiece of Steinhaus efforts has been the creation of a strong and vibrant public-private partnership with an emphasis on teamwork, a common community mission and building a more diversified economy. These strategies and policies to recover from the 1993 IBM downsizing and its impact have helped produce significant new job growth. Mr.
