clueweb09-en0001-25-34374.0 Laboratory Three: Epithelium II Laboratory 3: Epithelium II Reading: RR&K, Chapter 4 I. Objectives: Recognize the epithelial and nonepithelial components of an organ. Classify the epithelium that covers or lines an organ. Understand the characteristic features of epithelia at the LM and EM level. Recognize intracellular junctions at LM and EM levels and understand their functions. Understand the structure and function of the basement membrane. II. Summary of Slides to be Examined in this Exercise: SLIDE 56c, small intestine, rat (toluidine blue) FIGURE 03, small intestine, absorptive cell (EM) SLIDE 42c, skin, fingertip (H&E) SLIDE 77b, urinary bladder, monkey (H&E) SLIDE 42p, skin, carcinoma in situ, human (H&E) III. Microscope Slide Review SLIDE 56c small intestine, rat (toluidine blue) (RR&K p66, Fig.4.9) This slide contains a small fragment of the wall of the rat small intestine embedded in plastic. Similar to slides of the small intestine that you studied in the previous laboratory session,
clueweb09-en0001-25-34374.135 Laboratory Three: Epithelium II Similar to slides of the small intestine that you studied in the previous laboratory session, this slide shows several villi, which are lined by a simple columnar epithelium with a striated border. Observe the apical surface of the cells near the junction of the striated border with the rest of the epithelial cell. Identify small dark dots at the apical-lateral surfaces between adjacent epithelial cells. These terminal bars are best seen with the oil immersion lens while looking between cells that are cut perpendicular to the basement membrane. The small dots represent sections through the terminal bars. When the cut is parallel to the basement membrane and at the apical surface of the epithelial cells, the terminal bar appears as a dark blue line surrounding the cell. Be sure you can describe the components of the terminal bars that would be visible in transmission electron micrographs (see EM Package: FIGURE
clueweb09-en0001-25-34374.270 Laboratory Three: Epithelium II the terminal bars that would be visible in transmission electron micrographs (see EM Package: FIGURE 03). Nuclei (with nucleolus, euchromatin, and heterochromatin) are present at the basal portion of the epithelial cells while pale blue mitochondria are usually found near the apical region of these cells. Locate the goblet cells cut longitudinally and in cross-section. The deeply stained secretory product of these unicellular glands makes the goblet cells apparent. Can you find migrating lymphocytes within the epithelium that lines the villi? Observe the extracellular spaces between the absorptive cells. This space reflects the activity of fluid transport across the epithelium and is not an artifact of preparation. How do the terminal bars contribute to the formation of this extracellular space (or compartment)? Check list for SLIDE 56c: simple columnar epithelium simple squamous epithelium mesothelial cells endothelial cells striated border terminal bar terminal web goblet cells absorptive cells mitotic cells nucleus
clueweb09-en0001-25-34374.405 Laboratory Three: Epithelium II endothelial cells striated border terminal bar terminal web goblet cells absorptive cells mitotic cells nucleus nucleolus euchromatin heterochromatin mitochondria FIGURE 03 Small intestine, absorptive cell (EM) (RR&K p93, Fig.1,2) This electron micrograph represents the apical surface of two absorptive cells of the small intestine. The junctional complex demarcates the borders of these cells. Identify components of a junctional complex. Apical and lateral plasma membranes are visible. Identify the intercellular space between the lateral plasma membranes. The striated border seen at the LM is visualized here as numerous microvilli. Microfilaments (actin) can be seen projecting from each microvillus into a region of the cell void of organelles (i.e., terminal web). The small tubular vesicles beneath the microfilaments are endosomes, since these cells actively transport proteins. Check list for FIGURE 03: [.gif] zonula occludens zonula adherens desmosomes microfilaments mitochondria microvilli rough endoplasmic reticulum plasma membrane ribosomes lysosome terminal web endosomes SLIDE 42c
clueweb09-en0007-80-33169.0 Pap Smear HPV [Health Science Report] Site Navigation Home About Dr. Glickman Contact Us Disclaimer and Privacy The Greatest Time Prophecy in the Bible Research that helps: Home > Pap Smear > Pap Smear HPV Pap Smear HPV A pap smear-HPV positive, is common if one has had a history of vaginitis, or verruca. It is a test performed to check for signs of cancer of the cervix. During the test, the doctor takes a sample of cells from the cervix to be tested. The procedure is quick and painless. A pap smear-HPV positive, is common if one has had a history of vaginitis, or verruca. Just select from the Research Topics on the adjacent gray column for easy viewing of important articles. If this is your first visit, click here for a brief summary of this website. A Typical Cell Pap Smear , A Typical Pap Smear , Abnormal Cell In Pap
